Context: E526 treats the transverse vibrations of an elastic rod (Euler–Bernoulli beam). On the previous pages the normal-mode equation b c^4 y'''' = (1/k) y is integrated as y = α e^{s/f} + β e^{-s/f} + γ sin(s/f) + δ cos(s/f). Here k is the length of the isochronous simple pendulum, and f is fixed by f^4 related to b c^4 k. Each end can satisfy one of three conditions, each giving two equations:
- liber (free): y''=0 and y'''=0, i.e. zero moment and zero shear;
- simpliciter fixus (pinned or simply supported): y=0 and y''=0;
- infixus (clamped or built-in): y=0 and y'=0.

§14 (Scholion 2) lists the boundary-condition pairs for the ends E and F. There are 3×3 = 9 ordered pairs. Because the two ends may be interchanged, only 6 unordered pairs remain: free–free, free–pinned, free–clamped, pinned–pinned, pinned–clamped, clamped–clamped. Euler announces that he will treat these as six separate problems.

§15, Case I (free–free): a rod with no applied forces, lying on a perfectly smooth horizontal plane. The plane is there so that gravity plays no role. At s=0, y''=0 gives α+β−δ=0 and y'''=0 gives α−β−γ=0. At s=a, with ω=a/f, two further equations follow; they appear on the next page. Eliminating γ and δ there leads to the frequency equation cos ω cosh ω = 1. The root ω=0 is the rigid (non-vibrating) solution, and the nonzero roots give the free–free modes. This page only sets up the problem.
